"solarModuleFirmware": "Firmware",
"alarmhelp_solar_battery_overvoltage": "Means: The pack is above the voltage at which the battery's own protection acts, so it can cut the charge.\nCheck: The charge voltage set in the inverter or charger against the manual of the model (48 V models: 52.5 to 53.5 V), and that the battery and the inverter really communicate.",
"alarmhelp_solar_battery_low": "Means: The charge of the battery is low. A battery discharged down to its low-voltage limit switches itself off to protect itself.\nCheck: That the panels or the grid are charging it, and what the load takes. After a switch-off the manual says to charge it until the red light goes out (slowly, if it is very low).",
"alarmhelp_solar_battery_alarm": "Means: A module reports an alarm or a protection (red Alarm light blinking = alarm, steady = protection).\nCheck: The red light, the event log of the module here, and its temperature (charge only between 0 and 50 °C), current and voltage against the manual. If the red light stays on and nothing explains it, contact the distributor.",
"alarmhelp_solar_battery_hot": "Means: A battery is hotter than the limit set in the alarm levels. The manuals allow charging up to 50 °C and advise 10 to 40 °C.\nCheck: Ventilation (avoid closed spaces), distance from heat sources and from the air outlet of the inverter, and direct sunlight.",
"alarmhelp_solar_battery_cold": "Means: A battery is charging while colder than the low limit. The manuals allow charging only between 0 and 50 °C.\nCheck: Where the battery is installed: move it or warm the room, and limit the charge current of the inverter until it is back in range.",
"alarmhelp_solar_cells_unbalanced": "Means: The gap between the highest and the lowest cell is above the level set here (about 100 mV near full charge is a usual figure, not an official one).\nCheck: Let the battery reach a full charge: the manual says the BMS balances the cells while it cycles. If the same module and cell repeat in the extremes of the day, that module is the one to look at.",
"alarmhelp_solar_counter_rose": "Means: A protection counter in the history of a module went up: that protection acted. What each counter means is not official.\nCheck: Which counter it is (in the module's counters), and what the pack was doing then: charge voltage, current and temperature. The event log of the module shows the moment.",
"alarmhelp_solar_cell_soh_abnormal": "Means: The battery's own console calls the state of health of a cell Abnormal: that cell is the weak one of the module.\nCheck: The cell the alert names (counted from 1) in the bars of the module: if its voltage is low or it repeats in the extremes of the day, tell the distributor; the numbers are the console's, not an official verdict.",
"alarmhelp_solar_module_overcurrent": "Means: A module carries more current than the limit it states in its info. The manuals say its protection turns on above its maximum current.\nCheck: The charge and discharge current settings of the inverter or charger, the load, and how many modules share the current (more modules in parallel share it).",
"alarmhelp_solar_offline": "Means: The node no longer gets answers from this solar device (or the node itself is silent).\nCheck: The cable and its pins (RJ45 console: pin 3 battery TX, pin 6 battery RX, pin 8 ground), the speed (console 115200; an old unit may use 19200; RS485 with DIP switch 1: 1 = 9600, 0 = 115200). A battery may be asleep: the node's help says how to wake it.",
"alarmhelp_solar_battery_worn": "Means: The capacity the battery has learned is below the level set here against its rated capacity: it holds less than when new.\nCheck: The cycles and the health of each module: if one module is far below the others it is the one to replace; harsh temperatures shorten the life of the battery.",
